\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces The production spiral's collapse ($p=0.12$, $d=12$): level 5 from the restart at $t=36$ to the death by NaN at $t=60.44$, no panel involving the interior fill. Rules in every time panel: the handover at $t=36$ (solid), the merger transient at $t=41.2$ (dashed), the $\chi $ floor at $t=58.43$ (dotted). (a)~Separation of the two $\chi $ pits over the whole history---the level-3 leg carries the plunge from $d=11.9$, after which the tracker follows one merged pit (pale). (b)--(d)~$\min \alpha $ bottoms at $2.0\times 10^{-3}$ without reaching zero; $\min \chi $ decays exponentially onto its $10^{-8}$ floor at $r=0.078$; the scalar holds $\pm 0.89$ while $\Pi $ saturates at $\pm 4.1$---the profile survives, the momentum diverges. (e)~Growth compared, each curve $\log _{10}$-scaled onto $[0,1]$: the constraint norms peak at the merger and relax below where they started, while $\max |K|$ climbs only from $t=49$, with $\mathrm  {corr}(\log \max |K|,\log \|\mathcal  {H}\|)=-0.35$ after $t=40$---the death is not a constraint blow-up. Pale: the level-3 leg through the same transforms, median $3.5\%$ ($\mathcal  {H}$) and $0.7\%$ ($\mathcal  {M}$) over $t=36$--$50$. (f)~Radius of the $|K|$ spike (steps, drawn from $t=49.1$; earlier the profile is flat and an argmax is noise) and the $|K|>0.2$ edge (dashed), widest at $r=1.36$. Gold: the throat radius from the corrected scans---the spike rides just outside the throat and lands on it by the death. (g)~Throat areal radius $4.10\to 3.87$ over $t=55$--$60$, levels 3 and 5 agreeing to $0.2\%$, with \emph  {no} marginally outer-trapped surface at any scan. (The in-code $\theta _+$ columns are omitted: their naive $+r$ orientation manufactures trapped verdicts near a throat.) (h)--(j)~Radial anatomy at six equally spaced times, light ($t=36.1$) to dark ($t=60.4$): per-shell $\max |K|$ grows $0.08\to 6.1$ on a spike ${\sim }0.25$ wide migrating inward, while the $\chi $ and lapse pits stay at $r\lesssim 0.15$---the gauge collapse and the curvature wall are different places.}}{12}{figure.7}\protected@file@percent }
