\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces The waveform gallery: every channel's radiation, one row per scenario, one retarded clock. Left column: $r\,\mathrm  {Re}\,\Psi _4$ of the dominant mode at the innermost extraction sphere, each row on its own stated scale---the lone throat radiates an order of magnitude below the mergers, and the fly-by is the loudest channel of all. Half-height caps mark record ends: the collapsing throat's stream is junk-gated at $t=70$, the spiral is drawn to $t=60$---the end of its no-fill profiled leg, past which the record continues only behind a core frozen at $t=57$ (Sec.~\ref {sec:spiral:wall}), so the panel stops where the interior is still evolved---and the fly-by---complete to $t=100$, no horizon ever forming---is gated at $t=70$, ahead of the $t=76.1$ trough where the mouths' expansion (areal radii growing $\times 7.8$) has grown to equal the decaying burst at the $R=20$ sphere. Right column: the envelope $|r\Psi _4|$ at every extraction sphere on its own retarded time (ink to faint, inner to outer): a signal travelling at $c$ and falling as $1/r$ folds onto one curve. Gold dots mark the correlated wavefront, and the quoted $v/c$ is the lag of the whole complex waveform between neighbouring spheres over their common retarded window---peak-to-peak timing fails on flat-topped envelopes (the vacuum twin's merger plateau is ${\sim }15$ units wide at both spheres, and its peak times alone would read $v=0.57c$ where the waveform lag reads $0.82c$). Measured speeds: throat $0.91/0.95/0.98$, head-on $0.96/0.90$, spiral $0.95/1.00/1.00$, fly-by $1.00/1.00/1.00$, vacuum twin $0.82$. Every channel propagates at $c$ within the output cadence: radiation, not a gauge or constraint mode.}}{14}{figure.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:gw_gallery}{{9}{14}{The waveform gallery: every channel's radiation, one row per scenario, one retarded clock. Left column: $r\,\mathrm {Re}\,\Psi _4$ of the dominant mode at the innermost extraction sphere, each row on its own stated scale---the lone throat radiates an order of magnitude below the mergers, and the fly-by is the loudest channel of all. Half-height caps mark record ends: the collapsing throat's stream is junk-gated at $t=70$, the spiral is drawn to $t=60$---the end of its no-fill profiled leg, past which the record continues only behind a core frozen at $t=57$ (Sec.~\ref {sec:spiral:wall}), so the panel stops where the interior is still evolved---and the fly-by---complete to $t=100$, no horizon ever forming---is gated at $t=70$, ahead of the $t=76.1$ trough where the mouths' expansion (areal radii growing $\times 7.8$) has grown to equal the decaying burst at the $R=20$ sphere. Right column: the envelope $|r\Psi _4|$ at every extraction sphere on its own retarded time (ink to faint, inner to outer): a signal travelling at $c$ and falling as $1/r$ folds onto one curve. Gold dots mark the correlated wavefront, and the quoted $v/c$ is the lag of the whole complex waveform between neighbouring spheres over their common retarded window---peak-to-peak timing fails on flat-topped envelopes (the vacuum twin's merger plateau is ${\sim }15$ units wide at both spheres, and its peak times alone would read $v=0.57c$ where the waveform lag reads $0.82c$). Measured speeds: throat $0.91/0.95/0.98$, head-on $0.96/0.90$, spiral $0.95/1.00/1.00$, fly-by $1.00/1.00/1.00$, vacuum twin $0.82$. Every channel propagates at $c$ within the output cadence: radiation, not a gauge or constraint mode}{figure.9}{}}
