\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces The second radiation channel, on the same extraction spheres as $\Psi _4$. (a)~Energy through $R=30$ against time, each arm in units of its own gravitational-wave energy at the rule: the gravitational curve rises to $1$ by construction, the scalar curve falls to $-2.3$ (fly-by, solid) and $-2.4$ (spiral, dashed). The scalar energy is negative because gravity couples to minus this field's stress tensor, so an outgoing scalar wave carries negative energy and the bound system gains. (b)~Which multipole carries it: $\ell =1$ against $\ell =0$ and $\ell =2$ for the fly-by, the dipole a factor $10^{7}$ above both. Two opposite scalar charges radiate at dipole order; a vacuum binary has no such channel. (c)~Where the instrument stops: $|F_\phi |$ at both spheres against the times at which the inflating mouths' areal radius passes each sphere's own radius (grey rules). Left of the blue rule both spheres lie outside the mouths, and the energies in (a) are taken there; the same window read at $R=14$ gives $3.0$ in place of $2.3$, which is this measurement's error bar.}}{17}{figure.13}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:scalar_channel}{{13}{17}{The second radiation channel, on the same extraction spheres as $\Psi _4$. (a)~Energy through $R=30$ against time, each arm in units of its own gravitational-wave energy at the rule: the gravitational curve rises to $1$ by construction, the scalar curve falls to $-2.3$ (fly-by, solid) and $-2.4$ (spiral, dashed). The scalar energy is negative because gravity couples to minus this field's stress tensor, so an outgoing scalar wave carries negative energy and the bound system gains. (b)~Which multipole carries it: $\ell =1$ against $\ell =0$ and $\ell =2$ for the fly-by, the dipole a factor $10^{7}$ above both. Two opposite scalar charges radiate at dipole order; a vacuum binary has no such channel. (c)~Where the instrument stops: $|F_\phi |$ at both spheres against the times at which the inflating mouths' areal radius passes each sphere's own radius (grey rules). Left of the blue rule both spheres lie outside the mouths, and the energies in (a) are taken there; the same window read at $R=14$ gives $3.0$ in place of $2.3$, which is this measurement's error bar}{figure.13}{}}
