\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {14}{\ignorespaces A horizon, and only a horizon, shuts the ghost dipole off. Each curve is the \emph  {envelope} of $|F_\phi |$---a running maximum over $25$ units, one period of the dipole's own oscillation, smoothed in log amplitude---because the raw flux changes sign every ${\sim }20$ units and its zeros hide the amplitude on a log axis. (a)~The head-on arm (level 5 from $t=0$, scalar stream on) at $R=10/14/18$. The common MOTS closes at $t=21.5$ (dotted rule); the remaining hair leaves as one $\ell =1$ pulse, cresting later at each sphere, and then decays exponentially---dashed, log-linear fits over $t=30$--$95$ with $\tau =19/23/28$. This is the horizon-mass settling time of Fig.~\ref {fig:headon_collapse}(g), $\tau =19.4$. The rise before the rule is the two open mouths' static hair superposing, canonically ingoing and inside the near zone; only the post-horizon window is integrated. (b)~The control, each encounter on its outermost sphere---head-on $R=18$, fly-by and spiral $R=30$, so the colours are those of (a): the fly-by (no horizon, no merger) grows to the end of its record, and the $p=0.12$ spiral (merges, no MOTS on any scan) is still climbing at its $t=59.9$ wall (dashed rule). Past that wall the spiral is the frozen-core arm (dashed, half weight): its exterior agrees with the free arm to $0.1\%$ over their overlap, but a frozen source region carries no claim about its own decay.}}{18}{figure.14}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:scalar_censorship}{{14}{18}{A horizon, and only a horizon, shuts the ghost dipole off. Each curve is the \emph {envelope} of $|F_\phi |$---a running maximum over $25$ units, one period of the dipole's own oscillation, smoothed in log amplitude---because the raw flux changes sign every ${\sim }20$ units and its zeros hide the amplitude on a log axis. (a)~The head-on arm (level 5 from $t=0$, scalar stream on) at $R=10/14/18$. The common MOTS closes at $t=21.5$ (dotted rule); the remaining hair leaves as one $\ell =1$ pulse, cresting later at each sphere, and then decays exponentially---dashed, log-linear fits over $t=30$--$95$ with $\tau =19/23/28$. This is the horizon-mass settling time of Fig.~\ref {fig:headon_collapse}(g), $\tau =19.4$. The rise before the rule is the two open mouths' static hair superposing, canonically ingoing and inside the near zone; only the post-horizon window is integrated. (b)~The control, each encounter on its outermost sphere---head-on $R=18$, fly-by and spiral $R=30$, so the colours are those of (a): the fly-by (no horizon, no merger) grows to the end of its record, and the $p=0.12$ spiral (merges, no MOTS on any scan) is still climbing at its $t=59.9$ wall (dashed rule). Past that wall the spiral is the frozen-core arm (dashed, half weight): its exterior agrees with the free arm to $0.1\%$ over their overlap, but a frozen source region carries no claim about its own decay}{figure.14}{}}
